Slice 051, `tln tool remove talon` on Windows (R-RM5, R-ST10, R-HF11). The fake machine is `os=windows arch=x64`. The bin dir of Windows is `<USERPROFILE>\.local\bin`: the dir `u1\.local\bin` (a name with `\`) in the home. The files have `.exe`. One home for each run (`HOME` and `USERPROFILE` are `{w}/home/u<n>` dirs).

| Run | Home | Words | Result | Rules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | u1 | `tool rm talon --remove-tools` | option 2: the compilers, the tools (files, entries, `tools.json`), the user `Path` (one `exec!` of `powershell` with the script of `Setup.windows_path` `Remove`), the Talon data. Slice 050d (R-ST10): the tool files are not deleted, they are renamed. Under the lock of `tools.json`: one `random!`, `rename!` of the entry `foo.exe` and of the data file `foo.exe` to `foo.<hex>.del` (entry first), then `delete!` of `tools.json` only. Then Talon: one more `random!`, each running file is renamed to `<name>.<hex>.del` (`roc.exe`, `tln.exe`, then `<data bin>/tln.exe`), `roc.old.exe` goes back to `roc.exe`, and one detached `cmd.exe /d /c` waits and deletes the 5 `.del` files (`spawn_detached`, the files are in `TALON_DEL_1` to `TALON_DEL_5`: the 2 of the tool first). The `.del` files are in the tree: no delete call for them | R-RM5, R-RM6, R-RM8, R-ST10, R-HF11, R-ST4 |
| 2 | u2 | `tool rm talon --keep-all` | the entry `roc.exe` is another file (the user put it there): it stays with the warning, and `roc.old.exe` stays. Only 2 files are renamed (`tln.exe` and the data file), so the cleanup has 2 files. It does not start (`fx/exec`: `notstarted`): the renamed files stay, the warning on stderr names them, exit 0. Option 3: no PATH change (no `exec` call) | R-RM5, R-RM3, R-RM6, R-ST6, R-ST10 |

Slice 050c (R-ST10): each run on the fake Windows starts with the sweep of the `.del` files: `env_all` and one `list_dirs` of the bin dir and the data bin dir (no `.del` file here, so no `delete`). `calls.log` has these 2 lines before `args`.

Slice 088b (test world, 2026-11-02, R-LY5 to R-LY7): the fake Windows has `LOCALAPPDATA` and `APPDATA` in its env (HOME form of the case: `{w}/home` or the `u<n>` dir), so the roots are `<LOCALAPPDATA>\roc` (cache), `<LOCALAPPDATA>\talon` (data) and `<APPDATA>\talon` (config). In the case they are the dirs `AppData/Local\roc`, `AppData/Local\talon` and `AppData/Roaming\talon` (names with `\`, in `AppData/`); the packages dir is `AppData/Local\roc\packages`. The files and the paths in `expect/` follow this. The meaning of the runs did not change.

Slice 088f (R-LY10, Q216): `HOME`, `LOCALAPPDATA` and `APPDATA` of the fake Windows are absolute on Windows: `C:/` (HOME of a case with one home), `C:/AppData/Local`, `C:/AppData/Roaming` (with the `u<n>` home: `C:/u1/...`). They are real paths on the Linux and macOS host: `tests/commands/run.sh` makes the link `<W>/C:` to `home` for a case with `C:/` in `fx/`, and runs the app in `<W>`. So `C:/AppData/Local\talon` is the dir `home/AppData/Local\talon`, and the log has the portable text `C:/...` (no work dir path). A run of another system in the same case (the `fx/platform` rows) keeps its own env.
